winewayland: restack every client subsurface, not just the first 64 windows' (Lightroom Develop tips)

In Lightroom Classic's Develop module, after "Next" on the first help tip, the photo drew over the
tip, undimmed, and the tip could not be seen or closed; pressing Alt put it back until the next
"Next" (GitHub issue Neutron#5, follow-up). Measured in the clean room: the photo is a GPU child
deep in the main frame's tree of 240+ windows, and on every "Next" Lightroom destroys the
"Highlighter" window and creates a new one, which restacks the owner.

neutron_restack_apply orders the subsurfaces phase A collected from the Win32 tree, and that walk
stops at NEUTRON_ZMAX (64) windows. The photo's client subsurface was never in the list
(NT-EV restack ... zlist=64, photo absent), and a sibling the restack does not place ends up above
the whole chain it builds from the parent upward. So every restack lifted the photo over the dim
layer and the bubble; Alt only helped because a relayout re-placed the photo's subsurface just
above the parent.

Phase B now also asks the driver's own tree for every client subsurface attached to the toplevel
(wayland_client_subsurfaces_of, no user-lock calls) and places the ones phase A missed after the
client surfaces it ordered and before the owned windows, which float above every child in Win32.

Verified with a test program (100 child windows, a D3D11 child that presents and then stops, as a
still photo does, then dim + highlighter + bubble and a recreated highlighter): before, 0 px of
the bubble and 1.58M px of the photo on top; after, the bubble on top and the photo under the dim.
The first-run help stack test is unchanged. In Lightroom Classic (clean room, signed in, tips
reset), the photo no longer covers any tip; every restack lists the photo first.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

--- a/dlls/winewayland.drv/waylanddrv.h
+++ b/dlls/winewayland.drv/waylanddrv.h
@@ -570,6 +570,7 @@
 struct wayland_win_data *wayland_win_data_get(HWND hwnd);
 struct wayland_win_data *wayland_win_data_get_nolock(HWND hwnd);
 UINT wayland_owned_subsurfaces_of(HWND toplevel, HWND *out, UINT max);
+UINT wayland_client_subsurfaces_of(struct wl_surface *parent, HWND *out, UINT max);
 UINT wayland_owned_subsurfaces_zorder(HWND toplevel, HWND *out, UINT max);
 void wayland_restack_owned_now(HWND toplevel);
 void wayland_win_data_release(struct wayland_win_data *data);
--- a/dlls/winewayland.drv/wayland_surface.c
+++ b/dlls/winewayland.drv/wayland_surface.c
@@ -2804,6 +2804,33 @@
         if (zl->hwnd[i] == toplevel) top_idx = (int)n; else child_clients++;
         n++;
     }
+    /* NEUTRON (GitHub issue Neutron#5): the client surfaces phase A did not reach. Its tree walk
+     * stops at NEUTRON_ZMAX windows, and a sibling left out of this list is pushed ABOVE the whole
+     * chain below (place_above from the parent up) -- Lightroom Classic's Develop photo covered the
+     * help tips after every "Next" that way. They go after the client surfaces phase A ordered (the
+     * walk runs bottom of the z-order first, so the ones it misses are its upper end) and before
+     * the owned windows, which float above every child in Win32. */
+    {
+        HWND extra[NEUTRON_ZMAX];
+        UINT ne = wayland_client_subsurfaces_of(tsurf->wl_surface, extra, NEUTRON_ZMAX), e, j;
+
+        for (e = 0; e < ne && n < NEUTRON_ZMAX; e++)
+        {
+            struct wayland_win_data *d;
+            struct wayland_client_surface *cs;
+            BOOL listed = FALSE;
+
+            for (j = 0; j < n; j++) if (applied[j] == extra[e]) { listed = TRUE; break; }
+            if (listed) continue;
+            if (!(d = wayland_win_data_get_nolock(extra[e])) || !(cs = d->client_surface) ||
+                !cs->wl_surface) continue;
+            cs_of[n] = cs;
+            applied[n] = extra[e];
+            below[n] = cs->place_below;
+            if (extra[e] == toplevel) top_idx = (int)n; else child_clients++;
+            n++;
+        }
+    }
     /* NEUTRON: append the promoted OWNED windows LAST = top of the bottom-to-top order, which is
      * the Win32 rule (an owned window floats above its owner and the owner's children). Sourced
      * from the driver's tree because GW_OWNER is NULL for them -- see
--- a/dlls/winewayland.drv/window.c
+++ b/dlls/winewayland.drv/window.c
@@ -161,6 +161,35 @@
 }
 
 /***********************************************************************
+ *           wayland_client_subsurfaces_of
+ *
+ * NEUTRON (GitHub issue Neutron#5): every window whose client (GPU) subsurface is currently
+ * attached to `parent`, from the driver's own tree. Caller MUST hold win_data_mutex; this makes no
+ * user-lock calls, so it is safe in the restack phase-B context.
+ *
+ * The restack learns a toplevel's windows from a Win32 tree walk that stops at NEUTRON_ZMAX (64).
+ * Lightroom Classic's main frame has 240+, so its Develop photo -- a GPU child deep in that tree --
+ * was never in the list, and a sibling the restack does not place ends up ABOVE everything it
+ * does place: the chain is built from the parent upward. The photo then covered the help tips
+ * after every "Next" until something re-placed it. The driver knows every attached client
+ * subsurface regardless, so ask it.
+ */
+UINT wayland_client_subsurfaces_of(struct wl_surface *parent, HWND *out, UINT max)
+{
+    struct wayland_win_data *d;
+    UINT n = 0;
+
+    RB_FOR_EACH_ENTRY(d, &win_data_rb, struct wayland_win_data, entry)
+    {
+        if (n >= max) break;
+        if (!d->client_surface || !d->client_surface->wl_subsurface) continue;
+        if (d->client_surface->toplevel_wl_surface != parent) continue;
+        out[n++] = d->hwnd;
+    }
+    return n;
+}
+
+/***********************************************************************
  *           wayland_win_data_create
  *
  * Create a data window structure for an existing window.
